// endpoint_get_live_shell_runs_announced_before.proto —
// GetLiveShellRunsAnnouncedBefore: every detached SHELL run the record holds
// no terminal for whose announcement the store wrote before an instant.
//
// WHY THE SIDECAR ASKS. A process cannot outlive the kernel that ran it, so a
// shell announced before the machine's boot is ended, whatever the record
// says. A shell's terminal is the sidecar's, read off its spool, but a reboot
// empties /tmp, where the spools live, and the sidecar resumes each transcript
// at its committed cursor, past the launches it read before the reboot. Such a
// run is then on no file the sidecar reads and in no line it will read again:
// nothing would ever write its terminal, and the shim, for which a shell is
// live until the sidecar writes its end, re-announces it as running forever.
// The record is the one place that still names it, so the sidecar asks the
// record, once per process, for the shells announced before the boot, and
// concludes each LOST (swept_up) that no spool it reads accounts for.
//
// UNSCOPED ON PURPOSE. The sidecar reads every session on the host, and the
// question is about the machine's boot, which every session on the host
// shares; a run is answered with its owner, so each terminal is written into
// the right book.

